on a recent day at jasmine's caribbean  restaurant the staff were busy getting  ready for what they hoped would be a  bustling dinner crowd  the restaurant which opened a year ago  is in the heart of times square and  christmas and new year's are usually one  of the busiest times of the year  but with new york now the u.s epicenter  of the covet outbreak owner jasmine  gerard says there is a problem  for all business i mean we all are  suffering at this time right now i mean  for me thank the lord i was able to open  up in the pandemic and i had an  opportunity to do so but everyone is  really struggling and we definitely need  people to come back to new york city  last month the biden administration  reopened u.s borders to vaccinated  tourists for non-essential travel  visitors have trickled back into new  york city particularly times square  but not nearly to the pre-pandemic  levels  according to local officials about 270  000 people walk through times square  each day during the month of december  that's about 100 000 less than before  the pandemic  and about 25 percent of all the  businesses in times square remain closed  many are restaurants look closely on the  side streets and it's noticeably quieter  just on wednesday more than 43 thousand  people in new york city tested positive  for covet 19. the numbers are rising so  high so fast that officials have decided  to scale back the famous new year's eve  ball drop celebration here in times  square from seventy thousand spectators  to just fifteen thousand and everyone  will have to show proof of vaccination  and wear a mask i think we're cautious i  think that  at this point you know we're keeping an  eye on the positivity rate but it's not  worrying us to a point where we're  stopping back at jasmine's they've seen  this movie before having struggled  through last winter's covet wave we were  able to survive at that time and then  you know where them out spread  broadway open  but now it's the sequel and another  coved surge taking its toll on times  square  and on its biggest night of the year  [Applause]  gabriel zondo al jazeera new york
 

